            ]
        },
        "n-f3f870": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Papahanaumokuakea Marine National Monument ",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-a3d53a": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "When founded in 2006 it was the world's largest marine protected area, and is now also a <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://whc.unesco.org/en/list/1326/\" style=\"outline: none;\">UNESCO World Heritage site</a>. Many scientific studies here of coral and fish habitat are discovering new species. PHOTO of Red Pencil Urchin: <i>Wikimedia Commons</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-9bb1a5": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-e5a47d"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-fb555b": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-9bb1a5"
            ]
        },
        "n-4805ac": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Louisville Seamounts",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-e8bd1a": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "More than 20,000 large seamounts form a biome equivalent to the size of Europe, but only a few dozen have ever been extensively studied. A <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://ceoas.oregonstate.edu/features/tectonic/\" style=\"outline: none;\">recent expedition</a> on the scientific drillship <i> JOIDES Resolution</i> uncovered new findings. PHOTO: <i>A Koppers by permission</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-82b3cb": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-c4dd9b"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-ccd291": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-82b3cb"
            ]
        },
        "n-6a7d29": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Galapagos Islands",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-fc5d7f": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "An icon of wild places on land, the Galapagos is where important science takes place in the ocean too. It is also the focus of a new marine reserve as supported by <a href=\"http://www.seasketch.org\" target=\"_blank\" style=\"outline: none;\">SeaSketch</a>  IMAGE: <i>McClintock Lab, UCSB</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-738dec": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-0f2059"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-d98a16": {
            "type": "carousel",
