                "n-738dec"
            ]
        },
        "n-e83b18": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Giant Squid",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-29ff65": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Humans have been looking for the giant squid since it was first possible to take pictures underwater. The quest to see one alive remained a holy grail of ocean science & technology until scientists <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://news.nationalgeographic.com/news/2006/12/061222-giant-squid.html\">finally captured one on film</a> off the coast of Japan. PHOTO: <i>Wikimedia Commons</i> ",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-8f5b47": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-ff36a0"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-cd9526": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-8f5b47"
            ]
        },
        "n-e189be": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Tubeworm Barbecue",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-5c8254": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "This is where humans first encountered on April 14, 1991, the recent aftermath of an undersea volcanic eruption. Tubeworm specimens carried to the surface still had freshly charred flesh, hence the name of the site.  <a href=\"http://dusk.geo.orst.edu/oceans/tubeworm_bbq.html\" target=\"new\">READ MORE</a> PHOTO: <i>W Lange, D Fornari, WHOI by permission</i> ",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-2e575d": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-2a5bd4"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-32fece": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-2e575d"
            ]
        },
        "n-113d6b": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "\"Fire and Brimstone\" at Rota Volcano | <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://1.usa.gov/1a4nHe2\">VIDEO</a>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-43ada9": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "The <a href=\"http://n.pr/17sTSW3\" target=\"new\">first undersea eruption actually witnessed</a> in real-time by anyone was in 2006 on the NW Rota-1 volcano. Robotic vehicles with their cameras and sensors drew closer than anyone has ever been to ANY active volcano on land or undersea. IMAGE &amp; VIDEO: <i>NOAA by permission</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
