        "n-ce36b7": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Endurance Array",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-5a9e37": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "This site as part of the Ocean Observatories Initiative is one of the most comprehensive deployments of instruments and vehicles to study all aspects of the ocean from sea surface to seafloor. <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://www.whoi.edu/ooi_cgsn/page.do?pid=53332\">READ MORE</a> IMAGE: <i>U. Washington CEV by permission</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-81ca87": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-4cf652"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-3b99e3": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-81ca87"
            ]
        },
        "n-b0aada": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "South China Sea",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-55d76f": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "This is a very important place to watch politically, as well as scientificially.  The sovereignty claims of several nations are in conflict, but Singapore is an important peacemaker, and its <a href=\"http://www.fas.nus.edu.sg/geog/undergrads/minor_gis.htm\" target=\"new\">National University</a> is a leading adopter of GIS. IMAGE: <i>Wikimedia Commons</i>\n",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-5c7ad6": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-b9bc92"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-f1fb3b": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-5c7ad6"
            ]
        },
        "n-49f97b": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Southern East Pacific Rise",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-de31c0": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "This is where some of the fastest seafloor spreading occurs on Earth, leading to extensive underwater hotspring activity. The area was first examined with the same underwater vehicle that discovered the wreck of the <i>Titanic</i>. <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://dusk.geo.orst.edu/sepr/\">READ MORE</a> IMAGE: <i>U. New Brunswick by permission</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-ca2ee9": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-c8414a"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-ef801d": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-ca2ee9"
            ]
        },
        "n-5ff083": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Hauraki Gulf",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-391cba": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Esri supported UCSB in building SeaSketch as a new approach to marine spatial planning, based on the concepts of geodesign. <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://bit.ly/1gHK11S\">New Zealand government agencies are now using SeaSketch</a> to determine future uses of the Hauraki Gulf, where nearly every imaginable ocean user is fighting for space.",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-8a6e9f": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-7b1be6"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-4435bf": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-8a6e9f"
            ]
        },
        "n-f08103": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Pitcairn Island",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-2234f8": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "Pitcairn Island is well known as the place where the mutineers of the <i>HMS Bounty</i> settled in 1790, but a <a href=\"http://ocean.nationalgeographic.com/ocean/explore/pristine-seas/pitcairn/\" target=\"new\">recent scientific expedition</a> revealed the true bounty of these waters. IMAGE: <i>Wikimedia Commons</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-f3bce4": {
            "type": "image",
            "data": {
                "image": "r-bd4b33"
            },
            "config": {
                "size": "standard"
            }
        },
        "n-45b381": {
            "type": "carousel",
            "config": {},
            "data": {},
            "children": [
                "n-f3bce4"
            ]
        },
        "n-a5415c": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "h3",
                "text": "Wave Gliders a New Boon to Science",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
        "n-5830f6": {
            "type": "text",
            "data": {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "text": "The <a target=\"new\" href=\"http://bit.ly/164Ldu8\">Liquid Robotics wave glider</a> is the first to use ocean wave energy for propulsion. It recently collected data even in the middle of Tropical Cyclone Freda (a category 3 storm)! <a href=\"http://dusk.geo.orst.edu/Pickup/Esri/OcnSolns/chap16.pdf\" target=\"new\">RELATED GIS STUDY</a>   IMAGE: <i>Liquid Robotics</i>",
                "textAlignment": "start"
            }
        },
